Assistant: <think>What about graphing? We can see that the polynomial has at most two local extrema for $x \in \mathbb{R}.$ If it has only one real root, then both of them must be local maxima (or minima), and the graph must touch the x-ais at that point. That’s enough to nail down the values of $a$ & $b.$</ think/> <answer>5</ answer> <think>In 4-dimensional complex space, $P(x)$ has at most 8 real roots. Since there are exactly two such roots, with multiplicity 4, those two must be conjugate pairs. The way to track the graphs is to set $a = 2.$ But you could have very positive or very negative $a$ values that will have fewer real roots, but then there aren’t enough values of $z$ so that $P'(z) = 0,$ and that’s where things get complicated.

However, quadratics represent parabolas. You should be able to arguably see why there must be exactly two roots: it has only 1 or -1 as coefficients, but then the coefficient on $x^4$ is positive; in fact, that only places the same value of $x$ on both sides of the minimum of the parabola that determines two roots. Since $z = -2$ for $P'(x) = 0,$ the quadratic will always turn out with exactly one real root. </ think/> <answer>6</ answer>

